[{:seon.task/name "a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session global-ids (2026-10-23, notes/global-ids/MEASURED.md section 4) as a bound scalar planning 50x slower than a literal; measured by pure-tests, the scalar is not the cause. On a 116k-datom store the bound and the literal 3-clause point query cost the same (46k and 44k ticks); adding any clause on the form, [?f :form/order ?o] (or :form/ns, :form/id), costs 2.86M ticks, bound or literal, and grows with the store (1.17M at 47k datoms), while a fourth clause on the var costs 51k. The plan (datahike.query/explain) puts [?f :form/src ?src] [?f :form/order ?o] in an ENTITY-GROUP probed by the one ?f the first group binds. Cause: execute-plan-direct passed the consumer group's estimated output rows (:estimated-card g, 279) as execute-group-direct's :scan-estimate, whose rule seeks per probe value only when probe-set-size x probe-driven-threshold < scan size; against 279 that is false, so the whole of :form/src was scanned. Second cause: probe-driven-threshold was the JVM's 2500 (a ~50 us seek against ~20 ns per datom); on Jolt a probe's seek and merge is ~5.6k ticks and a scanned datom ~316, a break-even of N/18, so an attribute under 2,500 datoms was always scanned (329k ticks against 49.5k with the seek, a 12k-datom store). Fixed on Datahike jolt/in-scalar: deaf43a passes the scan op's own estimate at both call sites, 7de92bb makes the threshold 18. The proof's query: 3.24M -> 55k ticks at 10,000 forms."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by step E run #11 (2026-10-23, design run seon.context/e11-map-from-program#1, turn 3): `(d/q '[:find ?n :where [?e :ns/name ?n] [(clojure.string/ends-with? (str ?n) \"-test\")]] db)` answers no rows on a db holding 47 such namespaces, where binding first, `[(str ?n) ?s] [(clojure.string/ends-with? ?s \"-test\")]`, answers 47. Datalog takes no nested expression in a predicate (Datomic refuses the query), so the empty answer is a swallowed error: the model read it as no test namespaces and went exploring. Correct, in Datahike's query parser (a jolt/<topic> fork branch): a predicate or function clause whose argument is a list is refused with the clause named. Proof: that query raises, naming the clause.
Cause found 2026-10-23 (branch jolt/nested-call-refused): the parser reads (str ?n) as a constant list, -call-fn passes it to the predicate, and filter-by-pred reads the ClassCastException as false. Refused at the parse by datahike.query/refuse-nested-calls (Datahike dda24c0), a quoted form too unless it is q's query. The catch that read the error as false remains: task datahike.query/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.query-test/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused}} {:seon.task/name "a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (branch jolt/nested-call-refused) under task a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused: datahike.query/filter-by-pred wraps each predicate in a local safe-pred fn, which catches ClassCastException and IllegalArgumentException and answers false, so a predicate given a value of the wrong type ([(clojure.string/ends-with? ?n \"x\")] over symbols, a comparison of a string with a number) drops the row without a word, and the query reads as a true empty answer. A swallowed error (AGENTS section 9): what Datomic does (the query throws), whether any Datahike behaviour or test relies on the false (grep its tests for type-mixed predicates), then the fix at the producer: the error raised with the clause and the offending values, or, if a mixed-type column must filter, a typed comparison that never throws. Proof: a datahike.query-test test whose predicate meets a wrong-typed value raises, naming the clause. Fixed 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, Datahike jolt/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error 6b668f0, 25cc875, 698a2fa): both catch sites, filter-by-pred's safe-pred and the planner's post-filter-preds, now raise :query/call-threw (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw) with the clause, the call's arguments and the cause; a typed error the call threw passes on as is; the fused direct-rel, columnar and secondary-index paths, which caught every Exception as \"not applicable\" and fell back, rethrow it (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw?). Seon's process tests (seon.agent-test, seon.root-test, seon.repl-test, seon.main-test) pass, so no Seon query leaned on the false. Datahike's own query tests were run once on 6b668f0 and failed the same 11 as on d89be47; they were not re-run on 25cc875, because datahike.test.utils needs datahike.index.hitchhiker-tree, which is not on Jolt's source roots. The catch had also covered the built-in comparisons: their CollectionOrder protocol dispatched on the first value's type. Root's ruling (2026-10-23): they keep Clojure's semantics, so (< 1 \"a\") is the query's error, not a quiet answer. Datahike 698a2fa compares a number or nil as clojure.core/< does and any other value with compare. The planner pushes an ordering comparison into a range scan only when the store's schema is written and the bound fits the attribute's :db/valueType (datahike.query.analyze/of-the-values-kind?), so the scan, which would drop a value of another kind, answers what the predicate answers: [(> ?n 5)] over :ns/name symbols is now an AEVT scan whose filter raises, and [(> ?n proof.one-test)] stays an AVET range. On Jolt, clojure.string/ends-with? accepts a symbol (it reads its string), so the proof's predicate is pos? on a symbol."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.query-test/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error}}]
